Post-Viral Neuropathic and Sensory Dysfunctions
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Post-viral widespread burning pain, Severe mechanical allodynia, Patchy non-dermatomal sensory loss

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-038
Reason for Visit: Unbearable, widespread burning pain in the limbs preventing sleep following a recent viral illness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage recorded severe, diffuse burning pain and extreme sensitivity to touch, causing profound distress and total sleep deprivation. Urgent in-person evaluation is required to rule out acute myelitis or Guillain-Barré Syndrome variants, and to provide immediate neuropathic analgesia before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My arms and legs feel like they are sunburnt from the inside out. Even the breeze from the fan makes me want to cry."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a subacute onset of severe, diffuse neuropathic pain strictly following a systemic viral illness (fever, myalgias, cough) that resolved three weeks ago. The pain is characterized by widespread burning, tingling, and electric-shock sensations frequently lacking a strict dermatomal distribution, predominantly affecting the distal extremities. The patient exhibits profound mechanical allodynia, causing severe pain from normally innocuous tactile stimuli like the bedsheets. There is distinct hyperpathia leading to an exaggerated, prolonged pain response following a noxious stimulus. The patient describes patchy, asymmetrical areas of noticeable numbness affecting the fingers and toes. There is an exacerbation of the severe sensory symptoms occurring reliably at night, driving profound sleep disruption. Associated symptoms include severe fatigue and prominent central nervous system brain fog completely unresolving with rest.
Functional Status: Independent but severely distressed and sleep-deprived; pacing the floor to avoid touching surfaces.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme fatigue leading to potential accidents; profound psychological distress from unrelenting pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Visibly exhausted, agitated, wearing loose shorts and a tank top to minimize fabric contact with the skin.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals widespread, severe mechanical allodynia; light touch with a tissue elicits sharp pain withdrawal across the forearms and lower legs. Pinprick testing reveals patchy, asymmetrical areas of distinct sensory loss (hypoesthesia) in the distal fingers and toes, which does not conform to a single nerve or dermatome. Crucially, motor examination is completely normal (power 5/5 globally), ruling out significant motor demyelination. Deep tendon reflexes are symmetrical and preserved (2+). No ataxia. No spinal tenderness or signs of a sensory level.
Environmental Observations: Bed completely stripped of blankets; fan turned away from the patient.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen providing zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Small Fiber Neuropathy - Status: Acute Severe Pain Exacerbation
Differentials:

Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete preservation of motor strength, intact deep tendon reflexes, and the symptoms being entirely sensory (burning/allodynia) rather than ascending flaccid weakness.

Transverse Myelitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a distinct spinal sensory level, absence of upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia/spasticity), and intact bowel/bladder function.

Fibromyalgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, subacute onset directly tied to a viral illness, and the presence of severe, objective mechanical allodynia rather than just diffuse muscular tender points.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, generalized neuropathic pain and allodynia acutely following a viral illness, highly characteristic of a post-viral small fiber neuropathy. The intense pain and severe sleep deprivation prompted the urgent call. Delaying assessment would result in continued agony and extreme distress. Urgent after-hours physical examination definitively ruled out rapidly progressive, dangerous large-fiber/motor neuropathies (like GBS) or spinal cord lesions, safely diagnosing a pure sensory post-viral syndrome. This allowed for the immediate, crucial initiation of targeted, central-acting neuropathic analgesics, fulfilling the criteria for urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Amitriptyline 10mg orally STAT and 10mg at night ongoing. Prescribed a short course of Tapentadol 50mg PRN for severe, immediate breakthrough pain to ensure sleep tonight.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Step 1: By strictly wearing loose, 100% smooth cotton clothing and avoiding synthetic blends, the patient actively reduces microscopic friction on the skin, immediately decreasing the constant triggering of the allodynia.

Step 2: By applying cool, wet compresses to the burning limbs instead of using fans or ice packs, the patient safely numbs the hyperactive small nerve fibers without triggering a harsh temperature shock response.

Step 3: By taking the Amitriptyline exactly 2 hours before bed, the patient stabilizes the overactive central pain pathways, ensuring a solid block of restorative sleep, which is critical for nerve healing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to review medication efficacy and titrate if necessary.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if you suddenly develop profound weakness in your legs, become unable to walk, lose control of your bladder or bowels, or experience difficulty swallowing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; specialized skin biopsies are sometimes required by neurologists for definitive confirmation of small fiber loss.
Clinical Course: The nerve pain medications will take a few days to build up in your system, but sleep should improve tonight. Post-viral neuropathies frequently improve slowly over 6 to 12 months as the immune system settles.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if the burning pain fails to respond to the new medications after 72 hours, or if you develop a severe, spreading rash.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The recent virus caused your immune system to mistakenly attack the tiny, microscopic nerve endings in your skin. These damaged nerves are constantly misfiring, telling your brain you are burning when you are just being touched. Normal painkillers do not work on nerve damage. We are starting specific medications that act as a chemical brake to calm these misfiring nerves so you can sleep.
Actionable Steps: Take the Amitriptyline tonight. Wear loose cotton. Use cool, wet towels for relief instead of fans.

